														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1449638">
									

<p>My feeling is that the higher ups are wary of doing a decomm on this because they know about the infohazard aspect making people want to decomm it. And note that unlike some other, more virulent infohazards, it's actually pretty easy to avoid this one. All you have to do is tell the O5s: &quot;Knowing the full details of this item's effects gradually makes you subject to them. One verified non-contagious piece of information about this is that researchers are more likely to attempt to decommission it once they are subject to the effects. We strongly recommend that any decommissioning requests or attempts are presumptively denied pending review by an unaffiliated, unaffected 3rd party.&quot;</p>
<p>As for Keter, I was thinking &quot;no&quot;, because it's actually fairly well contained at the moment, and isn't trying to break out. Its effects are fairly well known (if not fully understood), and there are effective containment/treatment procedures in place. There might still be some affected individuals out in the rest of the Foundation due to info leakage before the infohazard aspect was realized, but there's an effective treatment protocol for that, too.</p>

							</div>

